  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "After Barinov begs Louis to bake the family wedding cookies properly, Nastya congratulates newly promoted director Nikita and stops him from quitting by revealing what Katya just told her mother.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты готовь печенье по этому рецепту.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я тебя прошу, очень постарайся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "А почему так дорого?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Шесть коробок с лепестками.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Каждый цветок разбирали.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Поздравляю с повышением, Никита.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Не стоит, я все равно уволюсь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Почему ты хочешь уйти?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "С Катей ничего не получается.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Да ты что, не вздумай!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Катя говорила маме, что ты ей очень нравишься.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Ты серьезно?",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты готовь печенье по этому рецепту.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You prepare the cookies from this recipe.' In Russian, the idea of 'you' flows naturally into 'recipe' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я тебя прошу, очень постарайся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I beg you, try very hard.' Chef Barinov addresses the listener with the informal singular 'you' alongside 'try'. Nikita answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "А почему так дорого?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And why is it so expensive?' Nikita anchors the line on 'and', while 'expensive' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Шесть коробок с лепестками.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Six boxes of flower petals.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'six' before landing on 'petals'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Каждый цветок разбирали.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They picked apart every flower.' Notice how Russian expresses 'every' and 'to take apart' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Поздравляю с повышением, Никита.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Congratulations on the promotion, Nikita.' Nastya anchors the line on 'congratulations', while 'Nikita' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Не стоит, я все равно уволюсь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do not bother, I am going to quit anyway.' Nikita brings out the contrast between 'not' and 'is standing' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Почему ты хочешь уйти?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why do you want to leave?' Nastya raises the pitch on 'to leave' to turn the phrase into a direct spoken question. Nikita answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "С Катей ничего не получается.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Nothing is working out with Katya.' Nikita brings out the contrast between 'with' and 'nothing' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Да ты что, не вздумай!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Come on, do not even think of it!' Notice how Russian expresses 'yes' and 'even think of it' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Катя говорила маме, что ты ей очень нравишься.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Katya told her mom that she likes you very much.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'Katya' before landing on 'appeal to'. Nikita answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Ты серьезно?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you serious?' Nikita raises the pitch on 'seriously' to turn the phrase into a direct spoken question. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
